[12.641467, "o", "     1\t8.3 ‘join’: Join lines on a common field\r\n     2\t========================================\r\n     3\t\r\n     4\t‘join’ writes to standard output a line for each pair of input lines\r\n     5\tthat have identical join fields.  Synopsis:\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\t     join [OPTION]... FILE1 FILE2\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\t   Either FILE1 or FILE2 (but not both) can be ‘-’, meaning standard\r\n    10\tinput.  FILE1 and FILE2 should be sorted on the join fields.\r\n    11\t\r\n    12\t     $ cat file1\r\n    13\t     a 1\r\n    14\t     b 2\r\n    15\t     e 5\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t     $ cat file2\r\n    18\t     a X\r\n    19\t     e Y\r\n    20\t     f Z\r\n    21\t\r\n    22\t     $ join file1 file2\r\n    23\t     a 1 X\r\n    24\t     e 5 Y\r\n    25\t\r\n    26\t‘join’’s default behavior (when no options are given):\r\n    27\t   • the join field is the first field in each line;\r\n    28\t   • fields in the input are separated by one or more blanks, with\r\n    29\t     leading blanks on the line ignored;\r\n    30\t   • fields in the output are separated by a "]
[12.645846, "o", "space;\r\n    31\t   • each output line consists of the join field, the remaining fields\r\n    32\t     from FILE1, then the remaining fields from FILE2.\r\n    33\t\r\n    34\t* Menu:\r\n    35\t\r\n    36\t* General options in join::      Options which affect general program behavior.\r\n    37\t* Sorting files for join::       Using ‘sort’ before ‘join’.\r\n    38\t* Working with fields::          Joining on different fields.\r\n    39\t* Paired and unpaired lines::    Controlling ‘join’’s field matching.\r\n    40\t* Header lines::                 Working with header lines in files.\r\n    41\t* Set operations::               Union, Intersection and Difference of files.\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\t8.3.1 General options\r\n    44\t---------------------\r\n    45\t\r\n    46\tThe program accepts the following options.  Also see *note Common\r\n    47\toptions::.\r\n    48\t\r\n    49\t‘-a FILE-NUMBER’\r\n    50\t     Print a line for each unpairable line in file FILE-NUMBER (either\r\n    51\t     ‘1’ or ‘2’), in addition to the normal output.\r\n"]
[12.64599, "o", "    52\t\r\n    53\t‘--check-order’\r\n    54\t     Fail with an error message if either input file is wrongly ordered.\r\n    55\t\r\n    56\t‘--nocheck-order’\r\n    57\t     Do not check that both input files are in sorted order.  This is\r\n    58\t     the default.\r\n    59\t\r\n    60\t‘-e STRING’\r\n    61\t     Replace those output fields that are missing in the input with\r\n    62\t     STRING.  I.e., missing fields specified with the ‘-12jo’ options.\r\n    63\t\r\n    64\t‘--header’\r\n    65\t     Treat the first line of each input file as a header line.  The\r\n    66\t     header lines will be joined and printed as the first output line.\r\n    67\t     If ‘-o’ is used to specify output format, the header line will be\r\n    68\t     printed according to the specified format.  The header lines will\r\n    69\t     not be checked for ordering even if ‘--check-order’ is specified.\r\n    70\t     Also if the header lines from each file do not match, the heading\r\n    71\t     fields from the first file will be used.\r\n    72"]
[12.646169, "o", "\t\r\n    73\t‘-i’\r\n    74\t‘--ignore-case’\r\n    75\t     Ignore differences in case when comparing keys.  With this option,\r\n    76\t     the lines of the input files must be ordered in the same way.  Use\r\n    77\t     ‘sort -f’ to produce this ordering.\r\n    78\t\r\n    79\t‘-1 FIELD’\r\n    80\t     Join on field FIELD (a positive integer) of file 1.\r\n    81\t\r\n    82\t‘-2 FIELD’\r\n    83\t     Join on field FIELD (a positive integer) of file 2.\r\n    84\t\r\n    85\t‘-j FIELD’\r\n    86\t     Equivalent to ‘-1 FIELD -2 FIELD’.\r\n    87\t\r\n    88\t‘-o FIELD-LIST’\r\n    89\t‘-o auto’\r\n    90\t     If the keyword ‘auto’ is specified, infer the output format from\r\n    91\t     the first line in each file.  This is the same as the default\r\n    92\t     output format but also ensures the same number of fields are output\r\n    93\t     for each line.  Missing fields are replaced with the ‘-e’ option\r\n    94\t     and extra fields are discarded.\r\n    95\t\r\n    96\t     Otherwise, construct each output line ac"]
[12.64618, "o", "cording to the format in\r\n    97\t     FIELD-LIST.  Each element in FIELD-LIST is either the single\r\n    98\t     character ‘0’ or has the form M.N where the file number, M, is ‘1’\r\n    99\t     or ‘2’ and N is a positive field number.\r\n   100\t\r\n   101\t     A field specification of ‘0’ denotes the join field.  In most\r\n   102\t     cases, the functionality of the ‘0’ field spec may be reproduced\r\n   103\t     using the explicit M.N that corresponds to the join field.\r\n   104\t     However, when printing unpairable lines (using either of the ‘-a’\r\n   105\t     or ‘-v’ options), there is no way to specify the join field using\r\n   106\t     M.N in FIELD-LIST if there are unpairable lines in both files.  To\r\n   107\t     give ‘join’ that functionality, POSIX invented the ‘0’ field\r\n   108\t     specification notation.\r\n   109\t\r\n   110\t     The elements in FIELD-LIST are separated by commas or blanks.\r\n   111\t     Blank separators typically need to be quoted for the shell.  For\r\n   112\t  "]
[12.646187, "o", "   example, the commands ‘join -o 1.2,2.2’ and ‘join -o '1.2 2.2'’ are\r\n   113\t     equivalent.\r\n   114\t\r\n   115\t     All output lines—including those printed because of any -a or -v\r\n   116\t     option—are subject to the specified FIELD-LIST.\r\n   117\t\r\n   118\t‘-t CHAR’\r\n   119\t     Use character CHAR as the input and output field separator.  Treat\r\n   120\t     as significant each occurrence of CHAR in the input file.  Use\r\n   121\t     ‘sort -t CHAR’, without the ‘-b’ option of ‘sort’, to produce this\r\n   122\t     ordering.  If ‘join -t ''’ is specified, the whole line is\r\n   123\t     considered, matching the default operation of sort.  If ‘-t '\\0'’\r\n   124\t     is specified then the ASCII NUL character is used to delimit the\r\n   125\t     fields.\r\n   126\t\r\n   127\t‘-v FILE-NUMBER’\r\n   128\t     Print a line for each unpairable line in file FILE-NUMBER (either\r\n   129\t     ‘1’ or ‘2’), instead of the normal output.\r\n   130\t\r\n   131\t‘-z’\r\n   132\t‘--zero-termin"]
[12.646231, "o", "ated’\r\n   133\t     Delimit items with a zero byte rather than a newline (ASCII LF).\r\n   134\t     I.e., treat input as items separated by ASCII NUL and terminate\r\n   135\t     output items with ASCII NUL. This option can be useful in\r\n   136\t     conjunction with ‘perl -0’ or ‘find -print0’ and ‘xargs -0’ which\r\n   137\t     do the same in order to reliably handle arbitrary file names (even\r\n   138\t     those containing blanks or other special characters).  Note with\r\n   139\t     ‘-z’ the newline character is treated as a field separator.\r\n   140\t\r\n   141\t   An exit status of zero indicates success, and a nonzero value\r\n   142\tindicates failure.\r\n   143\t\r\n   144\t   If the ‘--check-order’ option is given, unsorted inputs will cause a\r\n   145\tfatal error message.  If the option ‘--nocheck-order’ is given, unsorted\r\n   146\tinputs will never cause an error message.  If neither of these options\r\n   147\tis given, wrongly sorted inputs are diagnosed only if an input file is\r\n   148\tfound to co"]
[12.646449, "o", "ntain unpairable lines, and when both input files are non\r\n   149\tempty.  If an input file is diagnosed as being unsorted, the ‘join’\r\n   150\tcommand will exit with a nonzero status (and the output should not be\r\n   151\tused).\r\n   152\t\r\n   153\t   Forcing ‘join’ to process wrongly sorted input files containing\r\n   154\tunpairable lines by specifying ‘--nocheck-order’ is not guaranteed to\r\n   155\tproduce any particular output.  The output will probably not correspond\r\n   156\twith whatever you hoped it would be.\r\n   157\t\r\n   158\t8.3.2 Pre-sorting\r\n   159\t-----------------\r\n   160\t\r\n   161\t‘join’ requires sorted input files.  Each input file should be sorted\r\n   162\taccording to the key (=field/column number) used in ‘join’.  The\r\n   163\trecommended sorting option is ‘sort -k 1b,1’ (assuming the desired key\r\n   164\tis in the first column).\r\n   165\t\r\n   166\tTypical usage:\r\n   167\t     $ sort -k 1b,1 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   168\t     $ sort -k 1b,1 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   169\t     $ join fil"]
[12.646464, "o", "e1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   170\t\r\n   171\t   Normally, the sort order is that of the collating sequence specified\r\n   172\tby the ‘LC_COLLATE’ locale.  Unless the ‘-t’ option is given, the sort\r\n   173\tcomparison ignores blanks at the start of the join field, as in ‘sort\r\n   174\t-b’.  If the ‘--ignore-case’ option is given, the sort comparison\r\n   175\tignores the case of characters in the join field, as in ‘sort -f’:\r\n   176\t\r\n   177\t     $ sort -k 1bf,1 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   178\t     $ sort -k 1bf,1 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   179\t     $ join --ignore-case file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   180\t\r\n   181\t   The ‘sort’ and ‘join’ commands should use consistent locales and\r\n   182\toptions if the output of ‘sort’ is fed to ‘join’.  You can use a command\r\n   183\tlike ‘sort -k 1b,1’ to sort a file on its default join field, but if you\r\n   184\tselect a non-default locale, join field, separator, or comparison\r\n   185\toptions, then you should do so consistently between "]
[12.646473, "o", "‘join’ and ‘sort’.\r\n   186\t\r\n   187\tTo avoid any locale-related issues, it is recommended to use the ‘C’\r\n   188\tlocale for both commands:\r\n   189\t\r\n   190\t     $ LC_ALL=C sort -k 1b,1 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   191\t     $ LC_ALL=C sort -k 1b,1 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   192\t     $ LC_ALL=C join file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   193\t\r\n   194\t8.3.3 Working with fields\r\n   195\t-------------------------\r\n   196\t\r\n   197\tUse ‘-1’,‘-2’ to set the key fields for each of the input files.  Ensure\r\n   198\tthe preceding ‘sort’ commands operated on the same fields.\r\n   199\t\r\n   200\tThe following example joins two files, using the values from seventh\r\n   201\tfield of the first file and the third field of the second file:\r\n   202\t\r\n   203\t     $ sort -k 7b,7 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   204\t     $ sort -k 3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   205\t     $ join -1 7 -2 3 file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   206\t\r\n   207\tIf the field number is the same for both files, use ‘-j’:\r\n   208\t\r\n   209\t     $ sort -k"]
[12.646482, "o", "4b,4 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   210\t     $ sort -k4b,4 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   211\t     $ join -j4    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   212\t\r\n   213\tBoth ‘sort’ and ‘join’ operate of whitespace-delimited fields.  To\r\n   214\tspecify a different delimiter, use ‘-t’ in _both_:\r\n   215\t\r\n   216\t     $ sort -t, -k3b,3 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   217\t     $ sort -t, -k3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   218\t     $ join -t, -j3    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   219\t\r\n   220\tTo specify a tab (ASCII 0x09) character instead of whitespace, use (1):\r\n   221\t\r\n   222\t     $ sort -t$'\\t' -k3b,3 file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   223\t     $ sort -t$'\\t' -k3b,3 file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   224\t     $ join -t$'\\t' -j3    file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   225\t\r\n   226\tIf ‘join -t ''’ is specified then the whole line is considered which\r\n   227\tmatches the default operation of sort:\r\n   228\t\r\n   229\t     $ sort file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   230\t     $ sort file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   231\t     $ join -t '' file1.sorted file2.sort"]
[12.646503, "o", "ed > file3\r\n   232\t\r\n   233\t   ---------- Footnotes ----------\r\n   234\t\r\n   235\t   (1) the ‘$'\\t'’ is supported in most modern shells.  For older\r\n   236\tshells, use a literal tab\r\n   237\t\r\n   238\t8.3.4 Controlling ‘join’’s field matching\r\n   239\t-----------------------------------------\r\n   240\t\r\n   241\tIn this section the ‘sort’ commands are omitted for brevity.  Sorting\r\n   242\tthe files before joining is still required.\r\n   243\t\r\n   244\t   ‘join’’s default behavior is to print only lines common to both input\r\n   245\tfiles.  Use ‘-a’ and ‘-v’ to print unpairable lines from one or both\r\n   246\tfiles.\r\n   247\t\r\n   248\tAll examples below use the following two (pre-sorted) input files:\r\n   249\t\r\n   250\t     $ cat file1                          $ cat file2\r\n   251\t     a 1                                  a A\r\n   252\t     b 2                                  c C\r\n   253\t\r\n   254\tCommand                              Outcome\r\n   255\t                                     \r\n   256\t-------"]
[12.646517, "o", "-------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n   257\t     $ join file1 file2              common lines (_intersection_)\r\n   258\t     a 1 A                           \r\n   259\t     $ join -a 1 file1 file2         common lines _and_ unpaired lines\r\n   260\t     a 1 A                           from the first file\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@a876ad673e54:/app# "]
